## 1. Introduction
Ammonia is an important industrial raw material for the synthesis of fertilizers, fibers, pharmaceuticals, and more [1,2]. At present, the industry-standard synthetic ammonia process is the Haber-Bosch method to combine the nitrogen from air and hydrogen directly under the extreme high-temperature (573–773 K) and high-pressure $100{-}200\mathrm{atm};$ condition. The reaction of the HaberBosch method is highly energy-intensive and energy-consuming, and produces extensive by-product nitrous oxide $(\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ [3,4]. Given the importance for reducing the emission of greenhouse gases, such as ${\sf N}_{2}0$ (following ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ), the development of green and sustainable new processes to synthesize ammonia is urgently needed [5,6]. The use of solar energy provides a robust solution for solving the problem of heavy fossil energy consumption and environmental pollution which used in traditional processes. In recent years, photocatalytic nitrogen fixation has attracted more and more attention to leverage the energyconsuming and environmental shortcomings of the Haber-Bosch method [7–10].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline frameworks composed of organic ligands linked to metal clusters, with porous channels and active sites. MOFs are widely used in catalytic separation and other fields [23–26]. In this work, we used Fe clusters to design a Fe-based metal-organic framework (Fe-abtc) coordinated with the visible light-responsive organic ligand $\mathrm{H}_{4}.$ abtc. We also examined another type of MOF by changing the metal cluster to Zr coordinated with the organic ligand $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ , denoted as Zr-abtc. Our results show that both synthetized Feabtc and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc photocatalysts have good nitrogen fixation ability with the yields of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ are $49.8\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{(cat.)}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , $3\bar{5}.7\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{(cat.)}}^{-1}.\mathrm{h}^{-\mathrm{i}}$ , respectively. In addition, we confirmed that the N element originates from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , through the $^{15}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ isotope experiments.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $296\%$ ), zirconium(IV) chloride $(\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4},\mathsf{\Sigma}_{\ge}98\mathsf{\quad\%})$ , iron(III) chloride $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ , ${\tt299}$ $\%$ ) and 5- nitroisophthalic acid $(\geq98\ \%)$ were purchased from Adamas-beta Reagent Co., Ltd. N, N- dimethylformamide (DMF, AR), formic acid (AR), anhydrous ethanol (AR) and methanol (AR) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Zinc (Zn, $299\%$ ) was purchased from Shanghai Hushi Laboratorial Equipment Co., Ltd. Deionized water was used in all the experiments.
## 2.2. H4abtc synthesis
$\mathrm{H}_{4}$ abtc was synthesized following the method demonstrated in Ref. [27] $2.1\ \mathrm{g}$ of 5-nitroisophthalic acid, $2.1\ \mathrm{g}$ of $Z\mathrm{n}$ powder, and $3.2~\mathrm{g}$ of NaOH were added to a flask containing $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of water. The mixture was heated under reflux for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling and filtering, the yellow solid product was acidified with $1\ \mathrm{mol/L}$ hydrochloric acid, processed by suction filtration, and dried to synthesize the orange solid product. Use DMF for recrystallization to obtain pure $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ .
## 2.3. Fe-abtc synthesis
1 mmol ${\mathrm{FeCl}}_{3}$ and 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ abtc were added to $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The mixture was stirred for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ and transferred to a polytetrafluoroethylene liner. The liner was then placed in a stainlesssteel reactor and heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After washed with DMF and methanol several times, the product was soaked in methanol for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . During this period, methanol was replaced twice a day (every $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ ). After that, it was dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}C$ to produce orange solid powders, which was denoted as Fe-abtc.
## 2.4. Zr-abtc synthesis
The sample was synthesized with modifications according to the method proposed in ref. [28] 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}}$ and 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ - abtc were added to the mixed solution containing $16~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and $12\mathrm{\mL}98\%$ formic acid. The mixer was stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, it was put into a stainless-steel reactor (as above) and heated at 130 $^{\circ}C$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was washed with DMF and methanol several times, and then soaked in methanol for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ during which methanol was replaced twice a day. After that, it was dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and orange solid powders were obtained, which was denoted as $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc.
## 2.5. Photocatalytic performance
The photocatalytic nitrogen fixation experiment was carried out at the room temperature. $2{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of photocatalyst was added to $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.12\mathrm{\mg/L\K_{2}S O_{3}}$ aqueous solution, ultrasonicated for 5 min. Nitrogen was bubbled $(100~\mathrm{mL/min})$ into the reaction solution and stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the dark. $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of the reaction solution was taken and filtered with a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ filter as the reference. The reactor was then irradiated with a xenon lamp (300 W), reacted for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the concentration of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ (as $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ was determined by indophenol blue spectrophotometry with sampling $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of the reaction solution every hour.
## 2.6. $^{15}N_{2}$ isotope experiment
$^{15}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ was used instead of the normal $^{14}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ for the reaction described above. The reaction solution was acidified with $1\mathrm{~M~}$ sulfuric acid solution and concentrated by evaporation. Then, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMSO $d_{6}$ was added and mixed.
## 2.7. In situ infrared experiment
Use a hydraulic press to press the sample $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ into thin slices and place it in the groove. Then vacuum the whole system to a negative pressure of $10^{-4}$ mbar, raise it to $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ with a heating rate of $8.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , and keep it warm for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . After the temperature is cooled to $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , turn off the vacuum pump. Begin with a background test and then introduce ${\sf N}_{2}$ and water vapor into the insitu reaction chamber which is under the full-band light source ( $2\mathrm{cm}$ away from the sample). Record the absorption spectrogram every 5 min, and record for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ in total.
## 2.8. Characterization
The crystals of the samples were characterized using an X-ray diffractometer (XRD, Bruker AXS D8). The microscopic morphology and element distribution of the samples were characterized by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, Zeiss Sigma500) equipped with an energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS). The surface area was measured by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET, ASAP 2460) analysis. The chemical composition of the samples was analyzed by the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR, Bruker 55FT-IR), and the elemental composition of the samples was characterized by the photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific KAlpha) using Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\P}$ rays. The thermal stability of the samples was measured by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Netzsch TG 209 F3 Tarsus). The absorption spectra of the samples were characterized by the UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS, Hitachi U3900). Fluorescence intensity was characterized by a spectrofluorometer, and electron spin resonance (ESR) signal was characterized by the electron spin resonance (ESR, Bruker A200). The room temperature transient state fluorescence spectra (Edinburgh FLS1000) were employed to analyze the lifetime of photogenerated electrons and holes.
Photoelectrochemical tests (PEC) were performed on a CHI660E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua, Shanghai, China) with a standard three-electrode configuration. A Pt plate was used as a logarithmic electrode, Ag/AgCl was used as a reference electrode, and the electrolyte was a $0.5~\mathrm{M}$ sodium sulfate aqueous solution. The light source used is a 300 W xenon lamp (PLS-SXE300, Beijing Perfect Lamp Co., Ltd.).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation of Fe-abtc, Zr-abtc photocatalysts
Fe-abtc and Zr-abtc were synthesized by hydrothermal method. The synthesis scheme is shown in the Scheme 1.
## 3.2. Morphology and structure
The morphological features of Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc samples were examined by SEM. Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc samples are cube and cuboid structures, respectively, as shown in the Fig. 1.a-b. The images representing elemental distribution of sample Fe-abtc in Figure S1 a and b show a uniform distribution of various elements, indicating that the metal-organic framework-type catalyst has been successfully synthesized. Fig. 1.c shows that the Fe-abtc photocatalyst has an obvious characteristic peak at $8.20^{\circ}$ determined by XRD. The XRD pattern of sample $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc shows an obvious characteristic peak at $7.07^{\circ}$ , which is consistent with that previously reported in the literature, [28] indicating that the sample was successfully synthesized by our modified synthesis method. To determine the structure of the sample containing the ligand $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ , Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was performed on the samples and the ligand $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ . As shown in Fig. 1.d, a strong absorption peak at $1696~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the carboxylic acid in the ligand. The characteristic peaks of stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ in the group and the absorption peaks of $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ bond near $1423\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1250\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the result of coupling between the $-\mathsf{O H}$ bending vibration and the O-CO bond stretching vibration in the carboxylic acid group. The spectra of sample Feabtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc display no absorption peaks in the vicinity of $1250~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which indicates that the carboxylic acid group in the ligand is combined with the metal to form a carboxylate. The infrared spectra of Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc show the absorption peaks at $1378~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1618~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are generated by the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching vibration of carboxylate. Furthermore, the characteristic peaks at $1567~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $776~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the $c{-}0$ characteristic peaks generated by the bond stretching vibration between the benzene ring and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond. The characteristic peak of Fe-abtc at $618~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is generated by the stretching vibration of the Fe-O bond. The $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bond is detected at $658~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the Zr-abtc sample spectrum, which proves that the ligand carboxylic acid is closely assembled to the metal to form a stable carboxylate metal oxygen cluster structure.
The Fe-abtc and Zr-abtc samples were characterized by XPS to determine their elemental composition distribution. All the XPS spectra in this experiment were corrected with C 1 s at 284.80 eV. Fig. 2.a shows that sample Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc are composed of Fe, C, N, O and Zr, C, N, O elements, respectively. Fig. 2.b presents the $\mathsf{F e}2p$ spectrum of Fe-abtc in which the characteristic peaks located at binding energies (BE) of $710.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $724.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})2p_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})2p_{3/2}$ , respectively, and the characteristic peaks at BE of 709.1 eV, 722.5 eV contribute to $\mathrm{Fe}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{T}})2p_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ $2p_{3/2}$ , respectively. [29] The remaining four groups of characteristic peaks are the satellite peaks of Fe, which indicates that iron mainly exists in the trivalent form. A large amount of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ can stabilize the metal-organic framework structure by binding with ligands, while a small amount of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ is considered to be the active site for the nitrogen reaction. In the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}}3d$ spectrum of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc (Fig. 2.c), there are two obvious peaks: the peak at BE $185.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to $Z\mathrm{r}$ $(\overrightarrow{\mathrm{IV}})3d_{3/2}$ , and that at BE $182.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to $Z\mathrm{r}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{T}})3d_{5/2}$ [30]. The Zr element mainly exists in the form of ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ . The C1s spectra of the two samples are similar, as shown in Fig. 2.d. There are mainly four peaks located at 284.4 eV, 285.1 eV, 286.1 eV and $288.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ which correspond to, $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ CAC, CAN and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , respectively. The C 1s spectra confirm that the $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ ligand is assembled in the metal-organic framework. Additionally, the XPS high resolution spectra of N and O were also detected. It can be seen from Fig. 2.e that the N 1s spectra of the two catalysts are similar which show two kinds of peaks by fitting. The characteristic peak at $400.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ corresponds to ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathrm{-N}}/{\mathsf{N}}{\mathrm{=N}}.$ . The O 1s spectrum shows (Fig. 2.f) that the characteristic peaks of Fe-O and $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonds are located at $529.8{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and $530.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively. The characteristic peak at $531.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ belongs to the $c{-}0$ bond of the ligand, and the $533.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ position belongs to the water adsorbed on the oxygen vacancies (OVs) of the catalyst. As shown in Figure S3, the $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ produced in the photocatalytic nitrogen fixation reaction was clearly demonstrated by the existence of oxygen vacancies (OVs) in the samples by the ESR tests $(\mathbf{g}=2.001$ ) [31,32].
To detect the specific surface area and pore size of the samples, BET specific surface area tests were performed on the Fe-abtc and Zr-abtc photocatalysts. The nitrogen adsorption-analytical isotherm curves in Fig. 2.g show that both photocatalysts exhibit type I curves without hysteresis loops. The specific surface areas of Feabtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc are $971.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $481.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively. Feabtc photocatalyst has a larger specific surface area and is confirmed via its higher reaction rate. The pore size distribution curves in the inset of Fig. 2.g indicate that both photocatalysts are microporous materials with a typical pore size around $0.42~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The larger specific surface area and smaller pore size are beneficial to the adsorption, reaction and diffusion of nitrogen. To test the thermal stability of Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc, TGA measurement was performed, as shown in Fig. 2.h. Fe-abtc lost $18.4\%$ of its mass reaching to 330 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc lost $25.1\ \%$ of its mass reaching to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Their losses were due to a small amount of DMF, methanol and water molecules absorbed in the air. The ligand skeleton structure in Fe-abtc was sharply decomposed at $330{-}384^{\circ}C$ with a mass reduction of $39.6\%$ and Fe-abtc degenerated to $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . The ligand framework in Zr-abtc was decomposed at $400{-}510^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ with a mass reduction of $30.4\%$ . The degenerated Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc became stable at $700{-}800^{\circ}{C}$ with a remaining mass proportion of $41.5\%$ and 40.5 $\%$ , respectively.
## 3.3. Optical characteristics
The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra detection was performed on the synthesized photocatalysts to examine the response of the samples to light. Fig. 3.a shows that sample Fe-abtc starts to absorb light at $700\mathrm{nm}$ and tends to stabilize at $470\mathrm{nm}$ , which is similar to the absorption spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [33]. Sample $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc starts to absorb light at $550~\mathrm{\nm}$ and the absorption is strongest at $320~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Pure $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ can only absorb high energy photons with a wavelength shorter than $250\ \mathrm{nm}$ [34]. The synthesized Zr-abtc photocatalyst has a wider photoresponse range than pure $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ . The introduction of ligands enhances the absorption of visible light by Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc photocatalysts. We calculated the band gap $(\mathtt{E_{g}})$ of the photocatalyst by using the K-M equation: $(\mathsf{\alpha h v})=\mathsf{k}(\mathsf{h v}-$ $\bar{\mathrm{Eg})}^{n/2})$ [35,36]. The sample Fe-abtc is a direct semiconductor, and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc is an indirect semiconductor. Fig. 3.b shows that the band gap of Fe-abtc is $2.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ of and that of Zr-abtc is $2.31\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The flat band potential $\left(\mathrm{E_{fb}}\right)$ of the samples was tested on electrochemical work to determine the conduction band potential $(\mathrm{E_{cb}})$ and valence band potential $(\mathrm{E_{vb}})$ of the photocatalysts. Fig. 3.c shows the curves of $1/C^{2}$ of both photocatalysts vs. potential and the slopes of those curves are positive, which indicates that these two photocatalysts are $n$ -type semiconductors [37]. By making a slant tangent to the curve and intersecting it with the X-axis of Fig. 3.c, we can obtain that $\mathrm{E_{fb}}=-0.79\:\mathrm{eV}$ and estimate $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{c b}}$ by $\mathrm{E_{fb}}{-}0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ as $\mathrm{E_{cb}}=-0.89\:\mathrm{eV}$ for the Fe-abtc photocatalyst. Following the same procedure, we obtained $\mathrm{E_{fb}=-0.86~e V}$ and estimated $\mathrm{E_{cb}}=\mathrm{-}0.96~\mathrm{eV}$ for the. Zrabtc photocatalyst. According to the empirical formula: $\sf E_{v b}=E_{g}+E_{c b}$ , we get the valence band of the Fe-abtc photocatalyst is $\mathrm{E_{vb}}=1.28\mathrm{eV}$ and that of Zr-abtc is $\mathrm{E_{vb}}=1.35\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{c b}}$ potential of the two samples is higher than the reduction potential of nitrogen to ammonia. Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) experiments were performed on the ligands and the synthesized photocatalysts to gain a deeper understanding of photogenerated carrier separation efficiency, as shown in Figure S4. Both Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc have smaller intensity than pure ligands, and Fe-abtc has the lowest photoluminescence intensity, which confirms that the sample Feabtc’s photogenerated carrier separation efficiency is higher. The fluorescence lifetime decay curves of the samples (Fig. 3.d) were fitted by a double exponential model. The average fluorescence lifetimes of Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc at $375\ \mathrm{nm}$ are 1.07 ns $(\tau_{\mathrm{av,~\Fe}})$ and 0.87 ns $(\uptau_{\mathrm{av},\textrm{Z r}})$ , respectively. It is known that the faster the electron-hole pair recombination is, the shorter the fluorescence lifetime and the lower the photocatalytic conversion efficiency are. The results show that sample Fe-abtc has a longer lifetime of charge carriers than Zr-abtc, which is beneficial to photocatalytic nitrogen fixation. Electrochemical photocurrent and impedance measurements were performed on the samples as shown in Fig. 3.e-f. It can be also seen that sample Fe-abtc has a higher photocurrent and a smaller half arc than sample $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc. As such, relative to Zr-abtc, Fe-abtc has a higher photocurrent intensity and a faster charge transfer efficiency, which makes it more efficient for nitrogen fixation [38].
## 3.4. Nitrogen fixation performance and catalytic mechanism
## 3.4.1. Photocatalytic nitrogen fixation performance
The photocatalytic nitrogen fixation experiments were carried out at room temperature and ambient pressure $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , 101 Mpa). A 300 W xenon lamp was used as the light source. The ammonia produced by the reaction was measured spectrophotometrically with indophenol blue. As shown in Fig. 4.a, the ammonia production rate of the Fe-abtc photocatalyst is $49.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{(cat.)}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , while the ammonia production rate of the $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc photocatalyst is $35.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{(cat.)}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ . The higher production rate of photocatalyst Fe-abtc relative to photocatalyst $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc is mainly due to the larger specific surface area, stronger photocurrent and faster carrier separation efficiency of Fe-abtc. As the control experiments, ammonia was not produced by both photocatalysts under the dark conditions. To check the source of N element in the produced ammonia, the $^{15}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotope labeling experiment was performed on sample Feabtc (Fig. 4.b), which confirms that the nitrogen element of ammonia was indeed from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ . The sample Fe-abtc has good stability through the cycle experiments (Fig. 4.c). The samples Fe-abtc and Zr-abtc were characterized by XRD and SEM after cyclic reaction. As shown in Figure S5(a,b) and Figure S6(a,b), the XRD characteristic peaks and SEM microstructures of the two samples are basically constant. Also, as listed in Table ST1, the ammonia production rate of our synthesized Fe-abtc photocatalyst is in the moderate range relative to other photocatalysts reported in the literature Refs. However, considering that Fe-MOF materials are inexpensive and easy to access, our designed photocatalysts in this work still show great promise for sustainable futures in the ammonia industry.
The apparent quantum efficiency test uses a $395\ \mathrm{nm}$ singleband light source for nitrogen fixation experiments, and the apparent quantum efficiency $(\mathsf{A Q Y}=0.56\ \%)$ of the Fe-abtc sample is obtained. The calculation formula according to the literature [39] is as follows:
$$
A Q Y(\%)=\frac{N_{e}}{N_{p}}\times100\%
$$
