## 3.4.2. Photocatalytic nitrogen fixation mechanism
In situ infrared measurements were carried out to study the mechanism of photocatalytic nitrogen fixation. After the catalyst was saturated with nitrogen adsorption under the dark condition, multiple peaks in the infrared spectrum changed with the turning-on of the light and the increase of the irradiation time. As shown in Fig. 5.a, among these peaks, the $(\mathsf{v}_{\mathsf{N-H}})$ stretching vibration and the adsorption-OH characteristic peaks (Fig. 5.a) are located at $3555~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3260~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The chemisorption characteristic peak of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ is located at $1648~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5.b). When the light source was turned on, the chemisorption of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ was enhanced, which is attributed to the activation of the photocatalyst under the illumination. The characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ at $1235\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the characteristic absorption peaks of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ at $1397~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1425~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1475~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ increased significantly as the illumination time was prolonged. The characteristic peaks of $N\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were detected at $1107~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1118~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which also gradually increased with the illumination time [28,40].
3.5. Interactions between light and photocatalytic particles
The interactions between light and the synthesized Fe- and ZrMOF photocatalytic particles were studied by using the field-only surface integral method [41–45]. In the simulations, a plane electromagnetic wave with time harmonic $\exp(-\mathrm{i}\omega t)$ was used to represent the sunlight in which $\omega$ is the angular frequency and i is the imaginary unit. The spectrum of wavelength, $\lambda=2\pi c/\omega$ where $c$ is the speed of light, around the visible light was screened from $210\mathrm{nm}$ to $800\mathrm{nm}$ with a step of $10\mathrm{nm}$ . The incoming plane wave travels along $z\mathrm{.}$ -direction as $\pmb{k}^{\mathrm{inc}}=(0,0,k_{0})$ with $k_{0}=2\pi/\lambda$ being the wavenumber, and its electric field polarizes along $x$ -direction as $\pmb{E}^{\mathrm{inc}}=\pmb{E}_{0}\mathbf{exp}(\mathrm{i}k_{0}z)$ with $\pmb{{\cal E}}_{0}=(E_{0},0,0)$ . The Fe-abtc photocatalytic particle was set as a cube and its refractive indices across the visible light spectrum were taken the same as $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [46], while the $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -abtc photocatalytic particle was set as a rectangle and its refractive indices across the visible light spectrum were taken the same as $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ [47].
In Fig. 6, the variation of absorption cross section $\sigma^{\mathrm{abs}}$ across the wavelength spectrum of sunlight is presented. The definition of $\sigma^{\mathrm{abs}}$ is
$$
\sigma^{\mathrm{abs}}=\int_{s}\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{Real}\Big[\pmb{E}^{\mathrm{tot}}\times\left(\pmb{H}^{\mathrm{tot}}\right)^{*}\Big]\mathrm{d}s
$$
Fig. 6.b-c show the distribution of the surface charge density, $q$ ; on the surface of the synthesized photocatalytic particle where $\pmb{q}=\pmb{E}^{\mathrm{tot}}\cdot\pmb{n}$ with $\pmb{n}$ being the unit normal vector on the surface pointing into the particle. The distribution of $q$ on the particle surface can illustrate the potential movement of charge carriers. As shown in Fig. 6.b, since the size of the Fe-abtc particle is much larger than the wavelength, the surface charges intend to accumulate at the edges and corners with high curvatures. As the size of the Zrabtc particle is comparable to the wavelength, the surface charges oscillate along the surface of the particle as shown in Fig. 6.c.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, two kinds of MOFs (Fe-abtc and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -abtc) with cubic structure were synthesized by a hydrothermal method using the ligand $\mathrm{(H_{4}a b t c)}$ for photocatalytic nitrogen fixation. The large steric hindrance ligand $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ abtc corresponding to visible light enhances the light utilization of photocatalyst, and increases the transport efficiency of small molecular substances such as ${\sf N}_{2}$ , which is conducive to the reduction of nitrogen to ammonia by photocatalyst. The photocatalytic nitrogen fixation efficiency of the best sample Fe-abtc is $49.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{(cat.)}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , and the apparent quantum efficiency efficiency is $0.56\ \%$ . The $^{15}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ isotope labeling experiment and in-situ infrared test proved the reliability of nitrogen reduction to ammonia. The interactions between light and the as-synthesized Fe-MOF and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF photocatalytic particles were investigated using a field-only surface integration method, demonstrating that the introduction of the ligand $\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{a b t c}$ expands the photoresponse range. This study utilizes the visible light responding ligand to prepare MOFs, providing new insights into the effective photocatalytic nitrogen fixation by using inexpensive MOF materials.
